WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.480
Ulcerative colitis is one of the most common
forms of inflammatory bowel disease

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.360
and is characterised by inflammation
of the colon and the rectum.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.880
The walls of the colon — also known
as the mucosa — become inflamed

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.480
and no longer provide an effective
barrier between the inner cavity

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.840
and the surrounding connective tissue, allowing
gut microbes to cross the epithelial layer.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.160
This causes inflammation of the mucosa and
leads to a raft of unpleasant symptoms

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.600
such as abdominal pain, diarrhea,
fatigue and rectal bleeding.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.580
There are several commonly used anti-inflammatory
and immunosuppressive drugs,

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.860
but they come with side effects and
aren't long-lasting for many patients.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.220
Currently, 15% of people with
ulcerative colitis will require surgery

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:50.280
to remove some or all of the
colon within 20 years of diagnosis.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.620
Luckily, new insights into
the immunology of this condition

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.580
are helping scientists develop
new effective treatments.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.500
One new class of treatments is aimed at
preventing the inflammation in the first place,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.240
by bolstering the physical
integrity of the colon's epithelium.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.880
This keeps the immune-triggering bugs
confined to the interior of the colon.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:14.100
Some of these treatments work by
exploiting a human protein called IL-22

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.700
which helps strengthen the epithelial layer.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.740
One new drug in development targets the
receptor for IL-22 on the epithelium itself,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.760
activating it and thereby initiating healing
of epithelial cells and the mucus layer.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.580
This drug is currently in phase two trials.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.400
Researchers are also trying to use a
plant product called indigo naturalis

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:39.620
which encourages immune cells in the area to
release IL-22, again healing the epithelial barrier.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.380
There are also new therapies emerging that prevent
immune cells from entering the affected area.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.180
where they might become activated
to invoke an inflammatory response.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.040
One way to do this is to inhibit proteins called
integrins which live on the surface of T cells.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.920
A new antibody drug that works this way stops
T cells from getting across the blood vessel wall

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.980
into the mucosa and also blocks
their binding to the epithelium.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.880
This drug appears to effectively
reduce pain and inflammation.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.660
Another group of drugs uses
a totally different approach,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.760
targeting key inflammatory signals
between different immune cells.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.860
For example, one new drug tampers with
the cellular machinery inside T cells

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:21.480
so that they no longer respond
to messages from macrophages.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.720
In a pivotal phase three trial, this drug
induced remission in over 40% of patients

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.940
and has now been approved for the
treatment of active ulcerative colitis.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.420
A final approach to treating this disease is to
restore the healthy balance of the gut microbiome.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.980
One way of doing this is to
use probiotic bacteria streams.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.740
Adding these commensal bacteria 
back into the intestine's gut community

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.360
has been shown to quell inflammation
and restore normal intestinal function.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.720
One particular probiotic, known as E. coli nissle,
has been shown to keep symptoms at bay

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:57.920
just as effectively as widely
used anti-inflammatory drugs.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.320
Fecal transplantation from healthy
donors is also being experimented with,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.060
although results to date are conflicting.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.560
At least one recent study on multi-donor stool transplantation has shown promising results however,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.920
eliminating symptoms in a quarter of patients.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.440
As new approaches to tackling
this disease come onto the market,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.800
the hope is that more patients will be free of symptoms
for longer and won't go on to need aggressive surgery.

